# Building a New Block

Blocks are the atomic unit of the composable hub. A block defines a candidate's capabilities and determines what employers see, what admin can filter, and what AvA recommends. Follow every step below when adding a new block.

## 1. Block Registry

Add a `BlockDefinition` entry in `src/lib/block-registry.ts`. This is the single source of truth.

- `id`: Convention is `{category}-{name}` (e.g. `driver-mvr`, `developer-portfolio`, `general-resume`)
- `categoryId`: Must match an existing `BLOCK_CATEGORIES` entry (`general`, `drivers`, `developers`)
- `appearsOnCareerCard`: Set `true` if employers should see data from this block
- `pageRoute`: The `PageType` string if the block has a full-page view, else `null`
- `employerRequestable`: Set `true` if employers can request this block from a candidate (see section 5)
- `requestLabel`: The CTA label (e.g. `'Resume'` → "Request Resume"). Required when `employerRequestable` is `true`.
- `completionField`: Career card field that hides the button when `true` (e.g. `'hasResume'`). `null` = always show.
- Add a `BlockColorSet` entry in `BLOCK_COLORS` for the hub grid tile accent

## 2. Block Component

Create `src/components/blocks/[BlockName]Block.tsx`. If complex, create a subfolder.

## 3. Candidate Shell Wiring

Register the block in `CandidateShell`'s lazy block map so the hub renders it. If `pageRoute` is set, add a route case in the shell.

## 4. Career Card Visibility

Blocks signal what sections appear on a candidate's career card.

- **`src/components/CareerCard.tsx`**: Section visibility is derived from `data.installedBlockTypes`. Pattern:
  ```tsx
  const blocks = data.installedBlockTypes || []
  const hasDriverBlocks = blocks.some(b => b.startsWith('driver-'))
  ```
  If your block adds a new career card section, gate it on the block type being present.

- **`src/app/api/employer/talent/[userId]/route.ts`**: Already returns `installedBlockTypes` from `hub_blocks`. No changes needed unless the block stores data in a new table that the career card needs.

## 5. Employer Outreach (Automatic)

Employer request buttons are **data-driven from the block registry**. When you set `employerRequestable: true` on your `BlockDefinition`, `CareerCardModal` auto-generates a "Request {requestLabel}" button. You do NOT manually add JSX.

### What to set in the registry

| Field | Purpose | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `employerRequestable` | Whether employers can request this block from a candidate | `true` |
| `requestLabel` | CTA text shown to employers ("Request {requestLabel}") | `'Resume'` |
| `completionField` | Career card field to check — hides button when complete | `'hasResume'` |

### What's automatic (zero code changes)

- **Request button** in `CareerCardModal` — loops `getRequestableBlocks()` and renders `ActionButton` per block.
- **Block auto-install** — the request API installs the block on the candidate's hub via `targetBlockType`.
- **Deep-link notification** — `actionUrl` routes to `/?onboard={pageRoute}` so bell-click navigates to the block.
- **Invite emails** — `send-invite-email.ts` reads `label` + `description` from the registry. No per-block template needed.
- **Request emails** — `send-admin-notification.ts` uses `blockLabel` from the registry for `block_request` types.

### What you still wire manually

- **`BLOCK_TO_SLOT`** in `CareerCardModal.tsx` — maps your block ID to a `CareerCard` action slot (`resumeAction`, `mvrAction`, `dotAppAction`). If your block needs a new slot, add a prop to `CareerCard`.
- **`validOnboardPages`** in `page.tsx` — add your block's `pageRoute` so `?onboard=` deep-links work after invite onboarding.
- Employer MVR purchases must NOT update candidate career cards (CRA compliance).

## 6. Central Admin

Admin is block-centric. Most things are automatic; dedicated admin tabs are the exception.

### Automatic (zero code changes)

- **Block pills** on the candidates table — `CandidatesTab.tsx` reads `BLOCK_DEFINITIONS` for labels and uses the `{category}-` prefix convention for colors. New blocks appear as pills automatically.
- **Category filter** — `blockFilter` uses the prefix convention (`driver-*`, `developer-*`, `general-*`). Standard-prefixed block IDs work without changes.
- **`/api/admin/users`** returns `installedBlocks` and `blockCategories` per user from `hub_blocks`. No changes needed.

### Manual (when needed)

- **Admin tab** — If the block has data admins need to manage (like DOT apps, MVR orders), add a dedicated tab in `AdminDashboardShell.tsx` with its own API route. Not every block needs this — only blocks with admin-reviewable artifacts.
- **Data columns** — The candidates table has artifact-count columns (resume count, DOT app count) that are hardcoded. Add a count column if your block produces a countable artifact admins track.

## 7. AvA Journey Integration

- **`src/lib/journey-progress.ts`**: Add a `BLOCK_JOURNEY_MAP` entry so AvA can track progress for this block.
- **`src/lib/ava-context.ts`**: The system prompt auto-detects block categories via prefix (`driver-*`, `developer-*`). No changes needed unless the block requires specialized AvA guidance.

## 8. Stores

Register the block's page in `src/stores/types.ts` → `PageType` if it has a full-page route.

## 9. Block-Owned Data

### Data tables

Block data lives in `block_*` tables (e.g. `block_driver_cdl`, `block_education`, `block_skills`). Each block declares which tables it owns via the `dataTables` field in its `BlockDefinition`.

| Table | Owned by | Shared across |
|---|---|---|
| `block_driver_cdl` | `driver-resume`, `driver-dot-application`, `driver-cdl-credentials` | Driver blocks that need CDL info |
| `block_driver_employment` | `driver-resume` | Driver employment history (CDL resume / DOT) |
| `block_driver_mvr` | `driver-mvr` | Employer MVR requests |
| `block_driver_emergency` | `driver-dot-application` | — |
| `block_driver_experience` | `driver-dot-application` | — |
| `block_education` | `general-resume`, `driver-resume`, `developer-resume` | Shared across all roles |
| `block_skills` | `general-resume`, `driver-resume`, `developer-resume` | Shared across all roles |
| `block_references` | `general-resume`, `driver-resume`, `driver-dot-application` | — |
| `block_dev_profile` | `developer-resume`, `developer-portfolio` | Developer blocks needing bio/employment |
| `block_dev_github` | `developer-github` | — |
| `block_dev_portfolio` | `developer-portfolio` | — |

### Where data lives (cheat sheet)

| Data type | Table | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Identity (name, email, phone, address, avatar) | `user_profiles` | Shared across ALL roles. Never duplicate in block tables. |
| CDL / license info | `block_driver_cdl` | |
| Employment history (driver) | `block_driver_employment` | JSONB `history` array |
| Employment history (developer) | `block_dev_profile` | JSONB `employment_history` array |
| Education | `block_education` | JSONB `entries` array |
| Skills | `block_skills` | JSONB `entries` array |
| Emergency contact | `block_driver_emergency` | |
| Driving experience | `block_driver_experience` | |
| MVR data | `block_driver_mvr` | |
| References | `block_references` | JSONB `entries` array |
| GitHub data | `block_dev_github` | |
| Portfolio links | `block_dev_portfolio` | |
| Developer bio/certifications | `block_dev_profile` | |
| Share token / share settings | `users` | NOT in any profile or block table |

### Access layer

All block data reads/writes go through `src/lib/block-data.ts`. Never query `block_*` tables directly from API routes — use the typed helpers:

```typescript
import { getCdlData, saveCdlData, getResumePrefillData } from '@/lib/block-data'

// Single-block read
const cdl = await getCdlData(supabase, userId)

// Block-to-block prefill (composite read)
const prefill = await getResumePrefillData(supabase, userId)

// Write
await saveCdlData(supabase, userId, { cdl_number: '...', cdl_state: 'OH' })
```

### Adding data tables for a new block

1. Create a migration: `supabase/migrations/XXX_block_[name]_table.sql`
2. Add typed read/write functions to `src/lib/block-data.ts`
3. Set `dataTables` on the block's `BlockDefinition` in `block-registry.ts`
4. If other blocks need this data, add a composite read function to `block-data.ts`

## 10. My Files Integration (MANDATORY)

**Every block MUST surface a file entry in My Files** once the user has started or completed work in that block. My Files is the universal progress tracker across the hub — it is how users see what they've done across every block.

### Rules

- **My Files always renders** on the hub (even for new users). It is permanent, not gated on specific block types.
- **Every block generates at least one file.** When a user starts work in a block, a row must appear in My Files. Examples:
  - Resume block → resume document
  - DOT application block → application file (in-progress or complete)
  - MVR block → MVR record (processing or complete)
  - CDL block → CDL credentials file
  - Portfolio block → portfolio link file
  - Skills block → skills snapshot
  - Education block → education record
  - GitHub block → GitHub profile link
- **File status must reflect block progress.** Use statuses: `empty`, `in-progress`, `processing`, `complete`. My Files is where the user tracks what's done and what's not.
- **The hub API (`/api/driver/hub`)** must return the block's file data so `MyFilesSection` can render it. When adding a new block, add its documents to the hub API response.
- **`MyFilesSection`** in `CandidateHub.tsx` must handle the new block's document type (icon, actions, status badge, view/edit navigation).
- **Do NOT skip My Files** when building a new block. If you are building a block and My Files doesn't show anything for it, the block is incomplete.

## 11. AvA Journey Integration (MANDATORY)

**Every block MUST have a corresponding AvA journey step** that is marked complete when the block's work is done, and AvA must have knowledge to track and guide the user.

### Rules

- **When you build a block**, you must add it to **both** My Files (see section 10) **and** the AvA journey.
- **Journey step:** Add an entry to `BLOCK_JOURNEY_MAP` in `src/lib/journey-progress.ts` for the block's `blockType`. The step's `status` must be derived from real progress data (e.g. "complete" when the user has saved the main artifact: portfolio URL set, resume uploaded, DOT app submitted, etc.).
- **Progress data:** Add any new completion signals to `BlockProgressData` in `journey-progress.ts` and populate them in `src/stores/journey-store.ts` from the hub store (or from the hub API response). The hub API is the source of truth; the driver hub store is synced from it via `syncDriverHubFromApi`.
- **AvA knowledge:** Ensure AvA's context (e.g. `src/lib/ava-context.ts`) and any assistant templates (e.g. `developer-brain-templates.ts`) know about the block so AvA can reference it, nudge the user to complete it, and mark the journey step when done.
- **Completion = done.** The journey step is "complete" when the user has finished the block's primary action (e.g. added portfolio URL, submitted DOT app, added first resume). Do not leave journey steps permanently pending for blocks that have a clear "done" state.

## 12. Employer Outreach Summary

Outreach is **registry-driven**. See section 5 for the full rules. Quick reference:

| Path | Auto-install? | Deep-link? | Block email? |
|------|--------------|------------|-------------|
| **Application invite** (`CandidateOutreach` → `/api/employer/invites`) | Yes (`/onboard/[token]`) | Yes (`?onboard={pageRoute}`) | Yes (`send-invite-email.ts` + registry) |
| **Talent request** (`CareerCardModal` → `/api/employer/talent/[userId]/request`) | Yes (API auto-installs `targetBlockType`) | Yes (`actionUrl` on notification) | Yes (`blockLabel` from registry) |
| **Recruit** (`CareerCardModal` → `/api/employer/talent/[userId]/recruit`) | No (application-only) | Yes (notification → `/applications`) | Generic email |

### Do NOT:
- Set `employerRequestable: true` without setting `requestLabel`
- Skip `validOnboardPages` in `page.tsx` when your block has a `pageRoute`
- Hardcode notification URLs — always derive from `getBlockDefinition(blockType).pageRoute`

## 13. Removing / Deprecating a Block

There is NO shortcut for removal. Every file touched by "adding a block" must be reversed, plus data cleanup. Follow every step.

### Code removal (reverse of the add checklist)

1. **`src/lib/block-registry.ts`** — Remove the `BlockDefinition` entry and its `BLOCK_COLORS` entry
2. **`src/components/blocks/[BlockName]Block.tsx`** — Delete the component (and subfolder if any)
3. **`src/components/app/CandidateShell.tsx`** — Remove the lazy import and route case
4. **`src/stores/types.ts`** — Remove the `PageType` value if this was the only block using it
5. **`src/app/page.tsx`** — Remove the `pageRoute` from `validOnboardPages`
6. **`src/components/employer/CareerCardModal.tsx`** — Remove from `BLOCK_TO_SLOT` if present
7. **`src/lib/journey-progress.ts`** — Remove the `BLOCK_JOURNEY_MAP` entry and any `BlockProgressData` fields only this block used
8. **`src/stores/journey-store.ts`** — Remove the progress data population for this block
9. **`src/components/hub/CandidateHub.tsx`** — Remove any `MyFilesSection` gating for this block's document type
10. **`src/components/hub/BlockIllustrations.tsx`** — Remove the illustration entry
11. **`src/lib/block-data.ts`** — Remove read/write helpers if no other block shares the tables
12. **Block store** (`src/stores/[block]-store.ts`) — Delete if exists

### Hardcoded touchpoint files (check EVERY one)

These files contain per-block logic that is NOT driven by the registry. When adding or removing a block, you MUST check each:

| File | What's hardcoded | Why it matters |
|---|---|---|
| `src/lib/journey-progress.ts` | `BLOCK_JOURNEY_MAP` — per-block journey steps | Missing entry = block has no journey progress |
| `src/components/hub/CandidateHub.tsx` | `MyFilesSection` — `blockType === '...'` checks | Missing check = block produces no file entry |
| `src/components/hub/BlockIllustrations.tsx` | Block ID → illustration component map | Missing = generic placeholder on hub tile |
| `src/components/employer/CareerCardModal.tsx` | `BLOCK_TO_SLOT` — block ID → CareerCard prop | Missing = request button exists but doesn't attach to a card section |
| `src/app/page.tsx` | `validOnboardPages` array | Missing = invite deep-link silently fails |
| `src/components/CareerCard.tsx` | `startsWith('driver-')` section gating | Only matters if adding a new **category** |
| `src/components/career-card/ProjectedCareerCard.tsx` | `switch(blockType)` per-section rendering | Missing = projected career card shows nothing for this block |
| `src/app/api/career-card/route.ts` | `switch` building career card sections | Missing = career card API omits block data |
| `src/lib/ava-chat.ts` | Per-block switch for AvA conversation behavior | Missing = AvA has no block-specific guidance |
| `src/components/ShareProfileCard.tsx` | `sectionTypes.has('...')` badge checks | Missing = shared profile card omits block badge |
| `src/components/HomePage.tsx` | Hero hex tile block IDs + labels | Missing = block not shown on marketing page |

### Data cleanup

- **`hub_blocks` rows** — Existing users may still have `block_type = 'removed-block'`. The UI must handle `getBlockDefinition()` returning `undefined` gracefully (show raw ID, not crash).
- **`candidate_requests.target_block_type`** and **`application_invites.target_block_type`** — Text columns that may still reference the old block ID. These are historical records; do NOT delete them.
- **`block_*` tables** — If the block's data tables are NOT shared by other blocks, write a migration to drop them. If shared, leave them.
- **Notifications** — Old notifications with `actionUrl` pointing to the removed block's page will land on the hub (graceful fallback). No cleanup needed.

### Do NOT:
- Remove a block from the registry without checking every file in the touchpoints table above
- Delete `block_*` tables that other blocks share (check `dataTables` on all `BlockDefinition` entries)
- Leave `BLOCK_JOURNEY_MAP` entries for blocks that no longer exist (causes phantom journey steps)

