## Critical Rules

- **`driver_profiles` and `developer_profiles` DO NOT EXIST.** They have been dropped. Any reference to them is a bug.
- **Blocks define capabilities.** Never use `users.role` as the source of truth for what a candidate can do. Read `hub_blocks` instead.
- **`users.role` is only meaningful for `employer`** (gates employer features). All non-employer users are candidates regardless of installed blocks.
- **No auto-role-setting from blocks.** Installing a driver block does not change `users.role`. Admin categorizes by block tags, not role.
- **Block ID prefix = category.** `driver-*` = drivers, `developer-*` = developers, `general-*` = general. This convention is load-bearing — admin filtering, career card visibility, and AvA context all rely on it.
- **All data goes through `block-data.ts`.** Each block owns its own `block_*` table. No monolithic profile tables.
- **Block data is user-scoped.** Every `block_*` table has a `UNIQUE(user_id)` constraint. One row per user per block data type.
- **Identity is separate.** Name, email, phone, address, avatar live in `user_profiles`, not in block tables. Block tables store role-specific data only.
- **Every block must surface a file in My Files.** See "My Files Integration" section above. A block with no My Files entry is incomplete.
- **Every block must have an AvA journey step** that completes when the block's work is done. See "AvA Journey Integration" section above. AvA must have knowledge to track and guide the user.
- **Outreach is automatic for requestable blocks.** Set `employerRequestable: true` + `requestLabel` on your `BlockDefinition`. The request button, auto-install, deep-link, and email all derive from the registry. See "Employer Outreach" (section 5 + 12).
